 they serve a number of purposes uh  metabolically  so you know they signal  you know they can work as a pest  deterrent and as a chemical signal  so they're aromatic oils um  certain plants have only a few  terpenes there's been well over 200 that  have been identified  in cannabis so this is one of the  reasons that so many of the different  varieties of cannabis and hemp have such  unique scent profiles  metabolically they're secondary  metabolites produced through the  mva mep pathways so these  pathways build these compounds from  repeating units of isoprene so these  uh units uh  determine whether there are monoterpenes  or diaterpenes or sequestoterpines  it's just merely a designation of the  number of units of isoprene  used in their metabolic pathway  and so you hear this term chemovar a lot  it's become fairly popular and i'll talk  about it in a little bit more detail  shortly but basically the idea behind  this is it's sort of  the chemical fingerprint of the strain  of  hamper cannabis so it's why  certain strains uh make you feel  or or smell a certain way so  it is an important part of the hymn  experience  now why why should we care about  terpenes um  they they play a very very critical role  in the the consumer experience  on a very interesting note a lot of the  winners from cannabis cups have  unique and robust terpene profiles  so a very good analogy that i heard  about this was just like you know it's  like the difference between  a four dollar box of wine and a 200  bottle of wine those alcohol  concentrations are almost going to be  identical but one of them provides  a much different experience right  so um just like you wouldn't judge these  wines by  how much alcohol is in them um people  tend to not  judge cannabis based off of the  thc cbd like you'll see that you know a  lot of times there's this focus on high  numbers but really truly the experience  comes from the interaction between these  analytes  and uh the terpenes so  this is this term is has been coined as  the entourage effect  and it's a synergistic uh interaction  between  terpenes and cannabinoids in the human  body so  there's been evidence that shows that  terpenes also interact with the  endocannabinoid system so it's not  too big of a leap of faith to  recognize that they play a part in the  overall experience  so there's thought that you know these  these compounds uh magnifying  therapeutic benefits um a lot of times  they'll heal peer people discuss why you  know what one string makes them feel a  certain way  and another one you know may make them  feel sleepy although that is a  purely qualitative  speculation right so this  it most likely has to do with the  interaction of these compounds  and their brain chemistry so the idea is  that the  sum of each one of these parts is  greater than the  the whole experience  all right now before we get into  analysis we're going to talk a little  bit about  the sample preparation one of the  biggest struggles  with uh analyzing  um hemp and cannabis  is the broad variety of matrices that  show up to our lab so  you know cbd from hemp is put into  everything imaginable from shampoo to  bath bombs  dog treats toothpicks  beef jerky we've seen  everything you can imagine come through  our doors  so this creates some problems um  what there's a term in analytical  chemistry called a matrix effect  so this is sort of everything but the  target analyte that you're looking for  which in this case are terpenes  and their interactions with your ability  to measure them  so sometimes this can amplify you know  give you a false  increase in values or it can diminish  the response from the instrument and  you get artificially lower values  so you know it uh sometimes it takes us  a little bit longer to get  right on point with with some of his  analyses so  sample preparation we use 500 milligrams  of sample  so in analytical chemistry the larger  the sample size that you have  the more accurate 