ly  probably heard of a liquid chromatograph  this is a gas command  so both of those technologies are used  to  separate out analytes  and then the detector that we use in our  analysis is called a mass spectrometer  so this allows us to get a mass spectrum  of the the targeting our lights  and it allows us to easily identify  the compounds that we're looking for so  i'll get into that  in a little more detail in a minute  so we're going to start with the sample  injection system  so you can see all the way on the right  this is a 40  unit carousel we have 110 unit  carousel in our lab which basically  allows us to run continuously  so the sample is put into a 22  milliliter vial and sealed  and then it's rotated into the auto  sampler oven  where it's baked for uh 10 minutes at  160 degrees celsius  so this allows us to inject only  the compounds that we want to so  majority of terpenes so at least the  ones that we look at boil below 160  degrees  so everything that in the sample that  boils above that will remain in the vial  so once all of these uh analytes are  volatized  we inject a specific volume  into the inlet that is uh moves into the  column  and so the alternative to headspace is  liquid injection  which is a much more messy  methodology so basically you can see on  the right of this picture  is a sample vial um  there's a lot of chlorophyll and other  compounds so there's well over a  thousand compounds  found in the cannabis plant  so not all of those we want to look at  um you can see in that injection liner  there  that's that's after only seven  injections  you can see all that uh sort of  nastiness caught in the lighter  so these need to be changed quite often  at least weekly i've heard from certain  labs they change them every 25 to 30  samples  so this kind of creates a production  nightmare  oh headspace is a much cleaner  technology  i've heard of labs not cleaning their  source  or doing any sort of maintenance in this  regard for multiple 