o the run time for 21 terpenes is about  10 minutes  um we're a little bit hindered by  the headspace so it's about a total  from sample injection to complete data  collection it's about 15 minutes  so we can run about 70 plus samples in a  day  and then 42 terpenes is a little longer  about 14 minutes  so once these samples they come off one  by one  we're able to collect spectral data  using our mass spectrometer so mass  spectrometry  is a technique that allows us to measure  uh the charge ratios or  mass to charge of individual ions so  once these are separated and they move  into the mass spectrometer  they come in contact with what's called  a source  so in this case it's electron impact  which creates these ions so it basically  splits them into pieces and then  we use the instrument that we use for  this  is a single quadrupole so it's four  rods that utilize a oscillating  electric field that only allows certain  masses through  so it helps us eliminate  significant noise from entering the  detector  which allows us to have a quick run time  and then also be able to um  definitively determine what analytes  we're looking at  so the detector in this case is an  electron multiplier tube  so it basically uh amplifies the signal  and then we're allowed we're able to  collect a mass spectrum  so on the right here you can see on the  top and bottom these are two typical  mass spectrums from different terpenes  so these vast spectrums are basically  like a chemical fingerprint  so we can tell we can run this against  a database library and determine  exactly which  terpenes are in our sample so even if  we're  not able to quantitatively provide a  value  we can also you know if we get other  hits that we're not looking for  we can still determine what those  turbines are  and so our calibration so terpenes  typically are linear across a broad  range  i think in this we typically run from  about  two parts per million up to five hundred  terpenes you know they rarely exceed  one to two percent by total weight fruit  and flour  um although with extracts things like  live resins this sometimes can be an  issue  so usually we use this 20 to 1 dilution  and  it's sufficient for about i would say 90  plus percent of our samples  the highest chirping concentration i've  seen in flower was four percent  so it does happen occasionally in that  case we just uh  dilute and run it again  um okay if if you guys have any  questions or comments or thoughts or  want to learn more about any of this i'm  i love talking about terpenes and  analytical chemistry so  please feel free to reach out to me my  email is adam.floyd  um thank you very much for attending my  seminar

